## Reviewer points not applied
- a#29 lakh/crore grouping. The checker parses hi amounts only with a Western comma. Part b’s own verdict already says the Western comma is not a fix row because the digits match the English. ACCEPTED-ROUND says the same.
- a#1 suggested प्रथम दृष्टांत. That is a weaker label than the first-instance court. Part b’s term, प्रथम न्यायालय, is used in the column and the bio.
- a#17 著作權. The gloss is the right Traditional Chinese for copyright, and trademark and patent on the same sentence already have theirs. Adding it is the one character vi does not annotate on that surface, so the four-language disclosure test fails. It was not added, and the test was not edited.
- a#38 and b#24: deleting U+200B, or promoting Q16–Q20 to headings, fails the block check. The visible prose was rephrased; the invisible characters stayed.
- a#49: deleting the difficulty row changes the table. The cells were rewritten so they do not say the claim is easy.
- b#23: merging the 013/015 fragments changes the block count. Each paragraph was completed in place.
- a#46’s diagnosis that “सदैव नहीं” answers nothing. The FAQ heading is a yes/no question, so that opening does answer it. The section body, whose heading is not that question, was the real problem. Both the FAQ answer and the section body were still made self-standing.
- a#30’s list of English firm and university names. ACCEPTED keeps those proper names in Latin. Roles and the government offices were translated, with the English name left in parentheses.
- b#14’s punitive-damages sentence without the digit 1. The checker counts that 1 against the English source, so 1 गुना stays.
